  "retired_capabilities": {
    "0.4.0": {
      "description": "Editing a file that enforces a safety gate no longer blocks the merge by itself. You now get a plain-language notice on the pull request (the engine-guard check's run summary and a checks-tab note) instead of a red check demanding the guardrail-ack label. The label still blocks \u2014 and still asks for your deliberate confirmation \u2014 for the rare killswitch changes: repointing where the engine fetches its own code or where it may write, switching team mode off, shrinking your protected-paths list, demoting a merge-blocking check, or deleting a gate outright. To make any path always-blocking again, add it to .engine/operator-guarded-paths.json \u2014 paths you declare there hold the merge for your label, exactly as before."
    },
    "0.6.0": {
      "description": "Two typed commands are gone: `engine-conduct` and `engine-tune`. Manage your codes of conduct and your tunable settings through `engine-setup` instead \u2014 its conduct and settings sections do exactly what those commands did; there is no alias, not even a hidden one, so a stale name fails plainly rather than quietly continuing a competing vocabulary. Separately, starting a NEW Build from the old v1 plan format is no longer offered: Build now uses the v2 plan format, which lets a Build's work items depend on one another as a small graph instead of a flat ordered list. A Build already in flight on the old format keeps working, and its plans still read \u2014 nothing you have running breaks. Resuming an in-flight old-format Build from its Issue asks for real continuation evidence: the Build's published handoff must already be on its pull request, so a freshly written Issue cannot pass as an old Build. To move an old plan onto the new format, run the coordinator's 'plan migrate-v1' verb; it rewrites the plan as an equivalent step-by-step chain, and because the rewrite changes the plan you approve it again before it starts. Routine (unattended) Builds use the new format too."
    }
  }
